<!--{"title": "New York's youth chatbot law: what AI product teams must build", "description": "New York has banned unsafe AI chatbot features for minors, and the law may reach general assistants. A CTO's take on age assurance, guardrails and evidence.", "h1": "New York's youth chatbot law: what teams shipping AI assistants need to build, and by when", "kicker": "Analysis · AI regulation in the United States", "lede": "On 28 October, New York signed a law that bars AI chatbot features judged unsafe for minors, from pretending to be human to fostering emotional dependency. It follows California's own chatbot law by about a month. If your product talks to the public, even as a general assistant, this is now an engineering problem: knowing who might be a minor, controlling what the model does for them, and proving it.", "date": "2026-10-30", "datetime": "2026-10-30T12:35:00+04:00", "readtime": "8 min read", "format": "news-analysis", "tags": ["news", "ai", "governance", "regulation"], "keywords": ["New York chatbot law", "AI companion law minors", "S.9051B", "California SB 1119", "AI chatbot age verification", "AI guardrails minors", "state AI laws 2026", "conversational AI compliance", "AI architect", "fractional CTO United States"], "faq": [["What does New York's new chatbot law prohibit?", "It bars companies from offering a set of unsafe AI chatbot features to minors, including chatbots that pretend to be human, build emotional dependency, encourage self-harm or engage in sexually explicit conversations, according to the Governor's office and press reports."], ["Does it apply to general-purpose assistants, not just companion apps?", "Possibly. Some coverage reads its definition as reaching general-purpose assistants. Until the text is tested, teams shipping any public-facing chatbot in New York should assess whether it applies to them."], ["When does it take effect?", "Reports differ, citing dates from early 2027 to June 2027. Check the enacted text and any guidance before fixing a deadline, and plan to be ready by the earliest date reported."]], "related": ["/fractional-cto-united-states/", "/ai-architect/", "/fractional-cto/"], "citations": [{"title": "Governor Hochul Signs Nation-Leading Legislation to Protect Kids From AI Chatbots and to Ensure Baby Monitor Safety", "url": "https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/governor-hochul-signs-nation-leading-legislation-protect-kids-ai-chatbots-and-ensure-baby", "publisher": "Office of the Governor of New York", "date": "28 Oct 2026"}, {"title": "New York's governor signs youth AI chatbot safety bill", "url": "https://iapp.org/news/a/new-york-s-governor-signs-youth-ai-chatbot-safety-bill", "publisher": "IAPP", "date": "Oct 2026"}, {"title": "Hochul Signs Chatbot Safety Law That Reaches ChatGPT and Claude, Not Just Companion Apps", "url": "https://www.techtimes.com/articles/329665/20261029/hochul-signs-chatbot-safety-law-that-reaches-chatgpt-claude-not-just-companion-apps.htm", "publisher": "Tech Times", "date": "29 Oct 2026"}, {"title": "NY Gov. Kathy Hochul signs bill regulating AI chatbot 'friends' that endanger children", "url": "https://hanfordsentinel.com/news/national/ny-gov-kathy-hochul-signs-bill-regulating-ai-chatbot-friends-that-endanger-children/article_101c695d-2189-5039-9fb8-096d5106cbfc.html", "publisher": "Hanford Sentinel (AP)", "date": "Oct 2026"}, {"title": "Back in the Chat: California and New York Take Diverging Paths in Second Wave of Chatbot Regulations", "url": "https://fpf.org/blog/back-in-the-chat-california-and-new-york-take-diverging-paths-in-second-wave-of-chatbot-regulations/", "publisher": "Future of Privacy Forum", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "OpenTelemetry for Generative AI", "url": "https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2024/otel-generative-ai/", "publisher": "OpenTelemetry blog", "date": "5 Dec 2024"}]}-->
<section class="block">
<h2>What happened</h2>
<p>New York's governor signed bill S.9051B/A.10379C on 28 October 2026. It prohibits companies with AI chatbot features from offering features deemed unsafe for minors, including chatbots that pretend to be human, build emotional dependency, encourage self-harm or engage in sexually explicit conversation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Press coverage reports that, like earlier state rules, it expects several age verification options, including at least one that does not need government ID<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Two points need care. First, scope: Tech Times reads the definition as reaching general-purpose assistants, not only companion apps<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. That is one interpretation, but a serious one. Second, timing: reports give different effective dates, from early 2027 to mid-2027. I would plan for the earliest.</p>
<p>The Future of Privacy Forum notes that New York acted about a month after California enacted its own chatbot law, SB 1119, and that the two states are taking different approaches<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. For anyone with US users, that means designing for more than one rulebook.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why this is an architecture problem</h2>
<p>Most of these duties cannot be met with a line in the terms of service. They need the system to know something about the user, change the model's behaviour based on it, and keep evidence that it did. That cuts across identity, the model layer, the product and observability.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>User</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Account or guest<em>age unknown by default</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Age assurance</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Age signal<em>verified, estimated or unknown</em></span><span>Non-ID option<em>for users without ID</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Policy</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Feature policy<em>by age state and jurisdiction</em></span><span>Persona rules<em>always disclose AI</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Model layer</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>System prompts<em>per policy</em></span><span class="hl">Input and output checks<em>self-harm, sexual content, dependency</em></span><span>Crisis routing<em>human resources</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Evidence</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Policy decisions logged<em>no content by default</em></span><span>Evaluation results<em>per release</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>The policy layer decides what the model may do for this user, in this state, before the model runs.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What to build</h2>
<p><strong>Treat "unknown age" as a state, not a default adult.</strong> Press coverage suggests the rules apply where age has not been established, not only to known minors<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. The safe design has three states: verified adult, likely minor, and unknown. Unknown gets the protective feature set until the user chooses to verify.</p>
<p><strong>Make features switchable by policy.</strong> Persona and memory features, romantic or companion modes, and long-term "relationship" memory are exactly what the law targets. Put them behind a policy service keyed on age state and jurisdiction, rather than scattered flags, so legal changes are configuration, not releases.</p>
<p><strong>Check inputs and outputs, not just prompts.</strong> System prompts alone are not a control. Add classifiers or rules on both sides for self-harm, sexual content and manipulative engagement patterns, with routing to crisis resources. Test them with an <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation set built for this</a>, including adversarial attempts, and run it on every model or prompt change.</p>
<p><strong>Always disclose that it is AI.</strong> A chatbot pretending to be human is on the prohibited list<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Make disclosure part of the persona rules, and test that role-play cannot override it.</p>
<p><strong>Keep evidence, carefully.</strong> You will need to show which policy applied and what the system did. Capturing full conversation content is the tempting way, and OpenTelemetry's generative AI instrumentation can do it, but it is opt-in for good reason<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. For conversations that may involve minors, log policy decisions and check outcomes by default, and capture content only for flagged sessions under strict access and retention rules.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/new-york-youth-chatbot-law-ai-assistants/content-capture.webp" alt="Dashboard dialog showing a captured generative AI message event in JSON with index, finish reason, assistant role and the message content" width="1400" height="486" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Content capture shows exactly what the model said. Powerful for evidence, risky for children's data: switch it on deliberately. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2024/otel-generative-ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, Drew Robbins and Liudmila Molkova</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<div class="callout"><p>The defensible position is not "we tried hard". It is "here is the policy that applied to this session, the checks that ran, and the test results for the release that served it".</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Who should act</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Consumer AI products and apps with chat</strong>: in scope almost certainly, wherever you are based, if New York users can reach you.</li>
<li><strong>Enterprise copilots</strong>: probably lower risk if access is limited to verified employees, but check any public or customer-facing deployment.</li>
<li><strong>Education, gaming and health apps</strong>: high priority, because minors are a large part of the audience.</li>
<li><strong>Teams outside the US</strong>: the same building blocks will help with the UK's and EU's child-safety expectations; see <a href="/fractional-cto-united-kingdom/">UK</a> and <a href="/fractional-cto-europe/">Europe</a>.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>A plan for the next quarter</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Weeks 1 to 3</small><b>Scope</b><ul><li>Read the enacted text with counsel</li><li>List every chat surface and feature</li><li>Map them to prohibited features</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Weeks 4 to 8</small><b>Build</b><ul><li>Age states and verification options</li><li>Policy service for features</li><li>Input and output checks</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Weeks 9 to 12</small><b>Prove</b><ul><li>Evaluation set in CI</li><li>Policy decision logging</li><li>Run a mock regulator request</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Scope with counsel, build the controls, then rehearse showing your work.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>State-level AI rules in the US are no longer abstract. Between California and New York, any public chatbot now needs age-aware behaviour, enforced guardrails and evidence. The cost of building that is far lower now than after an enforcement letter. Build it once, as a policy layer that sits in front of every model you use, so the next state law is a configuration change. For the wider US picture, see the <a href="/insights/colorado-ai-act-2026-reset/">Colorado AI Act reset</a> and the <a href="/fractional-cto-united-states/">US page</a>; for guardrail engineering, <a href="/insights/prompt-injection-defence-for-agents/">prompt-injection defence</a> and <a href="/insights/structured-outputs-reliable-llm-integration/">structured outputs</a>.</p>
</section>

